The nine typed admonitions
===========================

Each renders with a fixed, translated title. You do not supply the title.

.. note::

   Information the reader needs but that does not change what they do.

.. warning::

   Something that can break, lose data, or cost money if ignored.

.. tip::

   A shortcut or better way. Removing it costs nothing.

.. important::

   A precondition the reader must satisfy to succeed.

.. caution::

   Proceed, but slowly — the operation is easy to get subtly wrong.

.. attention::

   Redirects the eye. Rarely the right pick: prefer ``important`` (precondition)
   or ``warning`` (consequence), which say *why* you are being interrupted.

.. danger::

   Irreversible damage or a safety hazard. If nothing is destroyed, it is a
   ``warning``.

.. error::

   Describes a failure state, typically alongside its message.

.. hint::

   A nudge toward the answer without giving it. Almost always ``tip`` is meant.


Custom title
=============

When none of the nine names the thing, supply your own title:

.. admonition:: Measured 2026-01-01

   The generic form. Use it for a titled aside whose category is one-off —
   a measurement, a caveat, a decision record. Do not use it to work around
   picking the right typed admonition.

.. admonition:: Prerequisites
   :class: important

   ``:class:`` borrows another admonition's styling while keeping your title.


``seealso`` — the cross-reference aside
========================================

.. seealso::

   Not an admonition about *this* content: a pointer to other documents that
   the reader may want next. Renders as its own titled block.

Its body is normally a list of cross-references — shown here as source, since a
template has no real targets to point at and a dangling ``:doc:`` is a build
warning:

.. code-block:: rst

   .. seealso::

      - :doc:`/some/other/document`
      - :ref:`some-label`


``rubric`` — a heading that is not a section
=============================================

.. rubric:: Not in the table of contents

A ``rubric`` is a bold title with no section semantics: it does not enter the
toctree, does not get a label, and does not affect the document hierarchy. Use
it for a heading *inside* a section — the closing note of a table, the title of
a short list — where a real section heading would pollute the outline.


Nesting
========

Admonitions take arbitrary body content, including each other. Indentation
alone delimits them:

.. warning::

   The outer body.

   .. code-block:: bash

      command --that --illustrates

   .. note::

      A nested admonition. Legal, and almost always a sign the outer one is
      trying to say two things.


Selection table
================

.. list-table::
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 18 82

   * - Form
     - Pick it when
   * - ``note``
     - the reader should know this, but does nothing differently
   * - ``important``
     - there is a **precondition** — do this first or the rest fails
   * - ``warning``
     - there is a **consequence** — breakage, data loss, cost
   * - ``danger``
     - the consequence is **irreversible** or unsafe
   * - ``tip``
     - a better way exists; ignoring it costs nothing
   * - ``caution``
     - the step works but is easy to get subtly wrong
   * - ``error``
     - you are describing a failure state, usually with its message
   * - ``admonition``
     - the category is one-off and deserves its own title
   * - ``seealso``
     - you are pointing **out of** this document, not commenting on it
   * - ``rubric``
     - you need a heading that must stay out of the toctree

Two failure modes are worth naming, because both make the markup stop working:

- **Everything is a note.** When every aside is a ``note``, the reader learns
  the block carries no signal and skips all of them — including the one that
  said data would be lost. The typed names exist to spend attention where it
  matters.
- **Warning inflation.** A ``warning`` for something that merely surprises the
  reader trains them to read past the next one. If nothing breaks, it is a
  ``note`` or a ``caution``.
